WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
Allegations and confirmed instances of fakery
have surrounded the production of Man vs.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.980
Wild over the years, and there are even some
things Bear Grylls and his producers have

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.059
admitted to making up.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.300
So if you ever thought the show was unbelievable,
you were right.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.320
Let's take a look at all the reasons Man vs.
Wild is totally fake.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.200
Although Bear Grylls claims to be a survival
and bushcraft expert, a closer examination

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.750
shows he wasn't closely involved in that world
before starting his show.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.820
Which isn't to say he's completely clueless.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.099
Grylls was in the military as a member of
the U.K.'s Special Air Service for three years,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.180
and as part of that unit, he was trained in
combat survival skills.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.460
But Grylls didn't come to prominence for his
military exploits.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.980
Before his big TV break with Man vs. Wild
in 2006, he became famous for his widely-publicized

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:49.220
adventures, including climbing Mount Everest
at age 23, crossing the North Atlantic in

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.700
an inflatable boat, and hosting a dinner party
in a hot-air balloon at 25,000 feet.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.260
While these were no doubt grueling experiences,
they don't necessarily qualify somebody to

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.520
be a survival expert.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.040
When you compare Grylls' résumé to those
of fellow TV survival hosts Ray Mears and

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.540
Les Stroud, it becomes clear that his experience
pales in comparison to theirs.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.280
Mears has taught bushcraft skills since 1983,
to both private citizens and the military.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.140
Similarly, Stroud has served as a wilderness
survival instructor since 1990, and teaches

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.100
survival skills to the Canadian military.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:24.520
So while Grylls may look cool when he heads
into the wild, he's not necessarily the one

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.240
you want keeping you alive out there.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.840
The premise of Man vs. Wild is one man alone
against the elements, but on several occasions

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.159
those "elements" involved four walls and a
roof, along with room service, according to

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:37.980
survival experts who worked for the show.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.500
While filming an episode in the Sierra Nevada
mountains, Grylls enjoyed the nightly hospitality

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.490
of The Pines Resort at Bass Lake, a modern
lodge resort offering two on-site restaurants,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.000
a spa, and other amenities.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.780
During another episode, Grylls was supposedly
stranded on a desert island that turned out

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.110
to actually be a Hawaiian archipelago, where
he again spent his nights in a local hotel.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.960
"Sometimes you gotta know…

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:03.440
You alright?

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.940
Sometimes you gotta know when to turn back."

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.620
On many occasions, Grylls has presented dubious
survival techniques that aren't actually usable

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.320
in real life.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.519
While filming one episode in a sweltering
locale, Grylls claimed that you could squeeze

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.460
and drink water out of elephant dung.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.720
Survivorman star and bushcraft instructor
Les Stroud, who has filmed several episodes

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.440
of his show among elephants, went on record
during a Reddit AMA to debunk that technique.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.120
He wrote,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.840
"It is NOT possible to squeeze drinkable water
out of elephant dung...

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.200
Well, it is when your cameraman has soaked
it with bottled water."

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.900
In a real survival situation, many of the
techniques that Grylls casually illustrated

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.780
on Man vs. Wild could actually endanger people.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:45.599
And we're not even going to get into the urine
drinking incidents.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.480
The worst is even simpler than that.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:52.060
The two dangerous moves that Grylls most frequently
demonstrated on his show involved hurrying

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:56.219
to cross through dangerous terrain, and getting
himself wet when he doesn't have to.

00:02:56.219 --> 00:03:00.669
For those familiar with the show, you know
Grylls hardly ever walked anywhere, particularly

00:03:00.669 --> 00:03:01.669
when he was on a mountain.

00:03:01.669 --> 00:03:06.760
Instead, he ran, jumped, leaped, and swung
his way down cliffs, mountainsides, and crevasses.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.580
That may work for a video game hero, but in
a true survival situation, any injury could

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.480
drastically decrease your chances of making
it out alive.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.669
A simple broken ankle, miles from civilization,
could be a death sentence.

00:03:18.669 --> 00:03:21.540
The smart move is to take your time finding
the safest route.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.700
It may take longer, but it'll help keep you
alive if you're really in the wild on your

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.200
own.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.059
Grylls also seems to make a point of getting
wet at every opportunity.

00:03:30.059 --> 00:03:34.639
He's rappelled with vines down waterfalls,
gone for swims across frigid arctic waters,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.840
and gone down river rapids on a pile of plastic
tarp and branches he called a "raft."

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:43.260
Beyond the very real danger of drowning in
such situations, or injuring yourself in a

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.180
slip or fall, getting wet in the wild is one
of the worst things you can do.

00:03:47.329 --> 00:03:51.640
Hypothermia occurs when the body's core temperature
drops three degrees or more from normal, and

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.780
it can set in extremely quickly, especially
when you're wet.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.440
On numerous occasions, former crew members
have alleged that entire scenes on Man vs.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.140
Wild were faked for the viewing audience.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:06.780
In one example, a wild mustang that Grylls
attempted to catch and ride in one episode

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.340
was really a tame animal rented from a local
trekking lodge.

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:15.580
In another episode, Grylls leaped over a chasm
of flowing lava, which was actually an illusion

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.680
created by hot coals, lighter fluid, and a
smoke machine.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.030
In another episode, Grylls is shown constructing
a bamboo raft.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.460
It was later revealed that a hired expert
and the show's crew actually built the raft

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:30.660
for Grylls before dismantling it so he could
be shown reassembling it on camera.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:36.440
After controversy emerged in 2008 over the
show's less-than-honest presentation, Grylls

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.640
and Discovery both apologized for the fakery
deployed on the show.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:40.960
Grylls told the BBC,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:45.380
"If people felt misled on how the first series
was represented, I'm really sorry for that."

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.900
The Discovery Channel only went so far as
to admit that small, isolated elements of

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.550
the show were fabricated, promising viewers
that future seasons would be more transparent.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.360
To fulfill that promise, a disclaimer was
added at the beginning of each episode to

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:02.340
acknowledge the occasional use of support
crews or fabricated situations.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:07.260
But as much as they tried to diminish it,
the message is still the same: you just shouldn't

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.980
trust what you see on this show.

